"""
jugeo.python_runtime.generated_contracts.s03_registry_surfaces

theory2.tex Ch21 §21.3 — Registries as Global Constraint Surfaces.

A *registry* in Python is a global dispatch table that maps keys (types,
names, tags) to implementations.  In the JuGeo sheaf-theoretic framework,
a registry is a *covering family* in the Grothendieck topology of the
Python runtime site:

    { φ_i: U_i → U_base }_{i ∈ I}

Each covering morphism φ_i binds one key type U_i to a concrete implementation
U_impl(i).  The registry is *complete* (has a valid global section) iff every
open neighbourhood of U_base is covered by at least one φ_i.

Coverage (§21.3.1):
    A registry R covers a type universe T iff for every τ ∈ T there exists
    some φ_i with source(φ_i) ≤ τ in the type lattice.  Gaps — types in T
    with no covering morphism — generate ResidualObligation entries.

ABC surfaces (§21.3.2):
    An abstract base class is a constraint surface: it declares abstract methods
    that form the *interface obligation* φ_abstract, and each concrete
    subclass provides a covering morphism φ_impl: U_impl → U_abstract that
    must satisfy the gluing condition (all abstract methods implemented).

SingleDispatch surfaces (§21.3.3):
    functools.singledispatch registries map type → implementation.  The
    coverage fraction is the fraction of common Python types for which an
    implementation is registered.

DataclassField surfaces (§21.3.4):
    A dataclass's __dataclass_fields__ dict is a registry from field-name
    strings to Field descriptors.  The coverage fraction is the fraction of
    fields that have defaults (optional vs required).

Exports: RegistrySurfacesCoordinator, RegistrySurfacesAnalyzer,
         RegistrySurfacesWitness, RegistrySurfaceRecord, RegistryKind
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import abc
import dataclasses
import enum
import functools
import inspect
import logging
import threading
import time
import typing
import uuid
from dataclasses import dataclass, field, replace
from typing import Any, Iterator, Optional

logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)

